  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported behavioral and psychosocial data from a relatively small sample of 311 adolescents, which may introduce reporting bias.",
      "what": "This study analyzed self-reported active commuting to school (ACS) and related psychosocial outcomes for 311 Spanish adolescents aged 13 to 15. The researchers classified school neighborhoods using principal component analysis and hierarchical clustering across macro- and micro-scale built environment features. While individual environmental scales showed no significant independent associations with commuting behavior, the combination of a green, walkable macro-scale environment and a highly supportive micro-scale environment was associated with a 7.31 times higher odds of active commuting to school and lower perceived planning or psychosocial barriers.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that urban design interventions to promote active school travel cannot rely on macro-scale walkability or micro-scale streetscape improvements in isolation; rather, both scales must be improved simultaneously to significantly influence adolescent behavior and motivation."
    }
  },
